  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2>Why the body and not the story</h2>
      <p>When something overwhelming happens, the body prepares a response: fight, flee, brace,
      freeze. If the response cannot finish, because escape was impossible or you were too small
      or it simply went on too long, the preparation does not evaporate. It stays, as a chest
      that will not unclench, a jaw set at rest, a startle that fires at nothing.</p>
      <p>That is why insight so often fails to move these symptoms. You can understand the
      origin of your bracing perfectly and still brace. Somatic Experiencing goes at the
      unfinished response directly.</p>
      <h3>Titration, the part that matters</h3>
      <p>The work is titrated: slow, and in doses. This is not caution for its own sake. A
      nervous system that gets flooded learns that attention to the body is dangerous, and then
      the next attempt is harder than the last. Done at a pace your system can integrate, the
      same work widens your window of tolerance instead of narrowing it.</p>
      <h3>It tends to fit when</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>Shock trauma is in the picture: an accident, an assault, a medical event.</li>
        <li>You go numb, or flood, when you talk about hard things.</li>
        <li>Anxiety lives in your chest and stomach more than in your thoughts.</li>
        <li>Sleep does not restore you.</li>
        <li>Talking about it has stopped helping.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>What a session involves</h3>
      <p>Gentle attention to what is happening physically: temperature, pressure, breath,
      impulses to move. The first work is establishing a felt sense of safety, because nothing
      useful happens without it. From there you build the ability to notice a sensation, stay
      with it briefly, and come back out, which is the skill the whole method rests on.</p>
      <p>You are not asked to narrate the trauma. Many people find they say less in SE than in
      the therapy they have done before, and that this is a relief rather than a loss.</p>
      <h3>On the evidence</h3>
      <p>Honest version: Somatic Experiencing has randomized controlled trials supporting it for
      PTSD symptoms and a wider body of observational work, and it does not yet have the decades
      of replication behind the cognitive-behavioral therapies. It is a reasonable, researched
      choice, not a proven superior one, and anyone selling it as either settled science or
      nonsense is overstating their case.</p>
      <h3>Reem's somatic credentials</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>Somatic Experiencing Practitioner (SEP)</li>
        <li>NARM therapist, trained in BASE-P and Brainspotting</li>
        <li>LPC-S in Texas since 2009, license 66562</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
