<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about Somatic Experiencing</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>What is Somatic Experiencing?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Somatic Experiencing is a body-based trauma therapy developed by Dr Peter Levine. It
      treats a trauma response as something left unfinished in the nervous system rather than a
      problem of belief, so the work happens through physical sensation: what your body is
      doing, in small amounts, with regular returns to something steadier.</p>
      <p>You are not asked to retell what happened. Sessions move in deliberately small pieces,
      and that pacing is the method rather than a courtesy.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What happens in a Somatic Experiencing session?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>You are guided to notice what is happening in your body: sensation, breath, pressure,
      impulses to move. The therapist works first to establish a felt sense of safety, then helps
      you approach difficult material in small pieces and come back out again. You are not asked
      to retell the trauma.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What is titration in somatic therapy?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Breaking trauma work into pieces small enough that your system can process them without
      being overwhelmed or retraumatized. It is the central safeguard of the method, and it is
      why Somatic Experiencing usually feels slower than people expect.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is Somatic Experiencing the same as somatic massage or bodywork?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. Somatic Experiencing is psychotherapy and involves no touch in this practice, which
      works online. The word somatic means it attends to the body; it does not mean hands-on
      treatment.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How many Somatic Experiencing sessions will I need?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Shock trauma can shift in a handful. Chronic bracing laid down over years takes longer,
      because the goal is a wider window of tolerance rather than the removal of one symptom. It
      is reviewed together rather than fixed at the start.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Does Somatic Experiencing work over video?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes, and it is how this practice delivers it. The work tracks your own sensation rather
      than the room, so video is not the obstacle people expect. Both you and your therapist have
      to be physically located in the same licensed state during a session, which means Texas, or
      Arizona if you are working with Reem.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What is the difference between Somatic Experiencing and EMDR?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>EMDR reprocesses specific memories using dual attention. Somatic Experiencing works with
      the state of the nervous system and does not require a target memory at all. In this
      practice they are used together: Somatic Experiencing builds the capacity, EMDR does the
      processing once there is room for it.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
